What is CORS in Restful API’s 
=========================
→ CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a rule used by browsers to check whether a website is allowed to access data from another server.
→ CORS policy by default not allows to access the resource (api)e from  different websites
→ Even if we access different protocol (https) or different port number cors restrict the accessing the api

🔹 What is an Origin?
An origin is defined by:
Protocol (http / https)
Domain (example.com)
Port (3000, 5173, etc.)

🔹 Preflight Request
For some requests, the browser sends an OPTIONS request first (called a preflight request) to check permissions before making the actual API call.

🔹 Example
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost:5173
Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE
🔹 Important Note
CORS is enforced by browsers, not by REST APIs themselves.
Tools like Postman are not affected by CORS.

How to remove CORS restrictions RESTful API's?
===========================================
→ CORS restrictions can be removed by enabling CORS middleware in application
→Allow the frontend origin to access the REST API.
EX : 1 : app.use(cors({ origin: "http://localhost:5173"}));
EX : 2 : Allow Multiple Origins (Array)
app.use(cors({
origin: [
    "http://localhost:5173",
    "http://localhost:3000",
    "https://myfrontend.com"
  ]}));
